ustify the risk of bombing. The optimal move is to pass. This allows me to retain strength, and conserve the bomb.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has played a 2 to beat my Ace, showing a desire to keep control early in the game (Landlord has 17 cards left). My hand is decent with a Bomb (10s), a high 2, and a structural Triplet (JJJ), but I have several loose singles (3, 8) and pairs. Both Jokers are still unknown (likely split or with Landlord given the play of the 2).\n\nReason: Bombing a 2 in the early game when the Landlord has a full hand is generally not recommended unless you can clear your hand immediately or apply severe pressure. Doing so now wastes my strongest defensive tool (the bomb) and leaves me vulnerable to the unknown Jokers. It is better to preserve the bomb for a more critical moment or to stop a long run later. I will let the Landlord have this trick.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
